NAME
       mount.nfs - mount an NFS file system

SYNOPSIS
       mount -t nfs [-o OPTIONS] SERVER:/PATH DIRECTORY
       mount.nfs SERVER:/PATH DIRECTORY [-o OPTIONS] [-r] [-w] [-v]

DESCRIPTION
       mount.nfs is the helper that mount runs for file systems of type nfs
       and nfs4. You normally do not call it directly: "mount -t nfs ..." or
       an nfs line in /etc/fstab uses it. It comes with the nfs-utils
       package, which also provides showmount.

       RHEL 9 uses NFS version 4.2 when the server offers it, else falls
       back to older versions. The export is named SERVER:/PATH, where PATH
       is a directory the server exports (showmount -e SERVER lists them).

       Files on NFS keep the user and group numbers of the server, so a user
       must have the same UID on both machines to own their files. Root on
       the client is usually mapped to nobody on the server (root_squash).

       For a mount at boot, add _netdev so the mount waits for the network:

           utility.lab.example.com:/exports/shared  /mnt/shared  nfs  defaults,_netdev  0 0

OPTIONS
       -o OPTIONS
              Mount options, separated by commas.

       -r, -w Mount read-only or read-write.

       -v     Say more about what happens.

NFS OPTIONS
       vers=4.2, nfsvers=3
              Ask for this NFS version.

       rw, ro Read-write or read-only (the server may still refuse writes).

       sync   Write changes to the server at once.

       hard, soft
              hard (the default) keeps retrying when the server is down;
              soft gives up with an error.

       timeo=N
              Wait N tenths of a second before retrying.

       _netdev
              Mount only after the network is up (for /etc/fstab).

EXAMPLES
       List the exports, mount one and check:

           showmount -e utility.lab.example.com
           mkdir -p /mnt/shared
           mount -t nfs utility.lab.example.com:/exports/shared /mnt/shared
           findmnt /mnt/shared

       To mount home directories on demand, use autofs instead of fstab.

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), umount(8), showmount(8), nfsstat(8), autofs(8), fstab(5)
